    {
      "label": "Why It Works",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Safety data and morale, side by side."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Team Wins sits right next to the incident log instead of a separate tool, so celebrating progress never gets treated as an afterthought."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Geography you can actually see."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Real OpenStreetMap tiles at real coordinates make spatial patterns obvious at a glance — no squinting at a lat/lng table to spot a cluster."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "One validated palette, zero guessing."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Every color on this dashboard passed the dataviz skill’s CVD and contrast checks before shipping — not eyeballed, run through the validator."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "Approach & Process",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "End-to-End Ownership"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Owned end-to-end design for Fleet Dashboard, spanning the incident log, the hazard map, and the team-wins morale panel that engineers actually check every morning."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "One Taxonomy, One Dashboard"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Nothing re-mapped for the office."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The same subsystem × severity classification the cockpit tags with renders directly here — no separate office-side schema to keep in sync."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Validated Palette, Not Guessed"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Ran every color through the dataviz skill’s validator before shipping it:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Status colors (critical/serious/warning/good) distinct from any categorical series",
            "Severity always paired with a label — color never carries meaning alone",
            "Contrast checked against the actual dark panel surface, not assumed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Built From Real Fixtures"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Every panel reads from the same shape of data the cockpit would eventually stream:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Hazard hotspots plotted at real lat/lng on OpenStreetMap tiles, no API key needed",
            "Vehicle damage modeled as its own typed fixture, not hardcoded JSX",
            "Team-wins counters computed from the same incident list, not separate numbers"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Same Taxonomy, New Surface"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Extended the cockpit’s data model for the office context, not reinvented it:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "A live map for spatial pattern-spotting across hotspots",
            "A vehicle damage summary for maintenance planning",
            "A team-wins panel, deliberately kept separate from the safety metrics"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
